[2026-05-19T08:40:00Z] [SETUP/Ghidra version] Chose Ghidra 12.0.4 over latest 12.1 because ghidra_psx_ldr extension's latest build (2026.03.10) only ships through 12.0.4. Loader/version compatibility outweighs newest Ghidra.
[2026-05-19T08:40:00Z] [SETUP/inputs] Inputs are SLUS_005.10 (PS-X EXE), terrain DLLs (overlay code per level), terrain EXPs (level data archives, IFF FORM), SHELL.DLL/LOAD.DLL (UI overlays), various EXPs (vehicles, hud, common). Will import each DLL as a separate Ghidra program; EXP files are data not code so they are not imported into Ghidra (parsed by custom tools instead).

[2026-05-20T22:30:00Z] [DECOMP_PASS_3/FUN_8002d82c TOTALED string] Chose to inline literal "%s TOTALED!" rather than declare an extern for DAT_8006572c. The format string lives in PSX read-only memory at 0x8006572c; replicating that address on host adds an unresolved data symbol without behavioral benefit. Behavior is preserved because FUN_80053004 receives the format string by value (variadic arg), and the resulting buffer bytes are identical regardless of the format string's host address.

[2026-05-20T22:30:00Z] [DECOMP_PASS_3/FUN_8002d82c FUN_80017240 stub return type] Chose to stub FUN_80017240 as int64_t (returning 0 explicitly) rather than the panic_stubs default int return. The PSX caller treats v0:v1 as a 64-bit dot product; an int-return stub on x86_64 would leave rdx uninitialised, corrupting the upper half of the int64 read. With the int64_t return, both halves are zero -> the (s4 < 0) check in kind==2 short-circuits to "moving apart" -> sets bit 0x18000 and returns 1, which is a safe no-op for the unwired path.

[2026-05-20T22:30:00Z] [DECOMP_PASS_3/FUN_8002d82c q12 multiply representation] Chose the single-expression `(int32_t)(((int64_t)a * (int64_t)b) >> 12)` over an explicit MIPS-shaped multu + sign-correction sequence. They produce identical results bit-exactly: signed 64-bit product right-shifted by 12 truncated to i32. The C form is clearer and the compiler emits the equivalent x86_64 imul/sar sequence.

[2026-05-20T22:30:00Z] [DECOMP_PASS_3/FUN_8002d82c FUN_8002b940 linkage] Chose to remove `static` from FUN_8002b940 in vehicle_kill.c (rather than add a non-static wrapper) so vehicle_collision.c can call it directly. Original PSX code does not have C-level static; the qualifier was a previous-session encapsulation choice that broke cross-file reuse.
[2026-05-20T23:56:57Z] [DECISIONS] [FUN_8002efe0 mode-2 drift paths: MIPS checks nst>=0/<=0 BEFORE storing steer (bgez/blez then early-return), so we only update and continue if the steer actually crossed the neutral threshold. Preserved exactly. Mode-3 inputMul: when dir<0 and flags&0x100==0 MIPS clears v1 (maxInput) before 0x8002f570 store, so reverse without reverse-flag gives inputMul=0. Delta^2/24 for modes-4/5 steer: MIPS uses multu + magic-multiply (0x2aaaaaab) for signed div; equivalent to C integer / 24 for the value range (delta max 128, delta^2 max 16384). Confirmed match.]
[2026-05-20T18:00:00Z] [matrix_ops.c/FUN_8004c874 calling convention] The MIPS function FUN_8004c874 uses non-standard MIPS calling convention: takes vector in t0/t1/t2 (caller-saved), returns results in same registers. Chose to expose as C function with (int x, int y, int z, int *ox, int *oy, int *oz) signature rather than global state side-effects, because callers in FUN_8004c934 can trivially pass the values by value and read results via pointers. GTE MAC/IR side-effects are preserved (gte_SQR, gte_GPF execute internally).
[2026-05-20T18:00:00Z] [matrix_ops.c/FUN_8004c934 V0/V1 stash] FUN_8004c934 MIPS uses GTE V0/V1 registers as scratch storage to preserve row1 components across the first FUN_8004c874 call. Added gte_ldVXY0_i/gte_ldVZ0_i/gte_stVXY0_V0/gte_stVZ0_V0/gte_stVXY1_V1 to libgte.c to faithfully implement the mfc2/mtc2 sequence. Alternative of using plain local variables was rejected to preserve the faithful MIPS structure.
[2026-05-20T18:00:00Z] [matrix_ops.c/s_vnormal_tbl table values] Precomputed 64-entry int16 table using round(4096*sqrt(64/(i+64))). Values have not been byte-verified against EXE yet. Formula is the standard PSX VNormalS reciprocal-sqrt table; values match published documentation. Marked HIGH confidence pending binary verification.
[2026-05-20T18:00:00Z] [physics_integrate.c/FUN_800173fc RTZ shifts] The MIPS uses RTZ arithmetic right shift (bgez/addiu bias pattern). Implemented as `if (v < 0) v += (1<<n)-1; v >>= n` throughout. For >>6 shifts: bias = 0x3f. For >>7 shifts: bias = 0x7f. All 6 RTZ shifts verified against MIPS instructions.
